   $q$Kofi says $0.999\ldots$, with the 9 repeating forever, is a little less than 1. Which statement is correct?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Kofi is right, because there is always a gap of $0.000\\ldots1$.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-infinite-gap-exists"},{"text":"Kofi is right, because $0.\\overline{9} = \\frac{9}{10}$.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treated-repeating-as-terminating"},{"text":"Kofi is wrong, because $0.\\overline{9} = \\frac{9}{9} = 1$.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Kofi is wrong, because $0.\\overline{9}$ is more than 1.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-repeating-overshoots"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Kofi is wrong, because $0.\overline{9} = \frac{9}{9} = 1$.$q$, $q$If $x = 0.\overline{9}$, then $10x = 9.\overline{9}$, and subtracting gives $9x = 9$, so $x = 1$ exactly. The idea of a tiny gap fails because the 9s never stop, so no last digit exists where a 1 could go.$q$, 'draft', null)
